+<h4> <a name="provenance">Recognizing messages generated by this
+Postfix MTA </a> </h4>
+
+<p> A Milter cannot reliably determine from message or envelope
+content whether a delivery status notification was generated by
+this Postfix MTA, or whether it was received from outside Postfix.
+For this purpose, Postfix &ge; 3.12 provides the
+<tt>{postfix_internal_origin}</tt> macro. The value is one of the
+following: </p>
+
+<dl compact>
+
+<dt> <tt>bounce</tt> (message generated by this Postfix MTA) </dt>
+<dd> <p> The message is a delivery status notification. An empty
+envelope sender address indicates that the message is a notification
+for a message that was not delivered or that was delayed, or that
+it contains a "sendmail -v" or "sendmail -bv" delivery report; this
+is called a single bounce. A double-bounce envelope sender address
+indicates that the message is a postmaster copy of a single bounce,
+or a notification of an undeliverable single bounce. </p> <p> <i>
+Bottom line: signing the message would allow a receiver to distinguish
+it from a forged message. </i> </p> </dd>
+
+<dt> <tt>notify</tt> (message generated by this Postfix MTA) </dt>
+<dd> <p> The message is an SMTP session transcript. The envelope
+sender contains a double-bounce address. </p> <p> <i> Bottom line:
+signing the message would allow a receiver to distinguish it from
+a forged message. </i> </p> </dd>
+
+<dt> <tt>verify</tt> (message generated by this Postfix MTA) </dt>
+<dd> <p> The message is an address verification probe, generated
+this Postfix MTA. There is no benefit from signing the message as
+it will never be delivered. </p> <p> <i> Bottom line: Milters should
+not make any changes to such a message. </i> </p> </dd>
+
+<dt> <tt>null or empty</tt> (message not generated by this Postfix
+MTA, or internally forwarded by this Postfix MTA) </dt> <dd> <p>
+The message was received with SMTP, QMQP, the Postfix sendmail(1)
+command, or it was internally forwarded through an alias or dot-forward
+file. </p> </dd>
+
+</dl>
+
+<p> By default, this macro is listed in milter_connect_macros. This
+allows a Milter to decide early whether to "accept" a message without
+further inspection. </p>
 
